# ScriptHammer Environment Variables
# Docker Configuration
UID=1000
GID=1000

# ─── Multi-instance / A-B testing ────────────────────────────────────────
# Second instance needs ONE flag, not seven env vars:
#
#   docker compose -p scripthammer-b up
#
# The -p flag scopes containers/volumes/networks. Host ports auto-assign
# because docker-compose.yml defaults them to 0 (ephemeral). Find them:
#
#   docker compose -p scripthammer-b port scripthammer 3000
#
# Pin a port by uncommenting below. Pinned ports belong to whichever
# instance reads this .env — all others must stay ephemeral or they
# collide on the pin.

COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=scripthammer
# Pin the dev port for a stable URL across restarts/self-heals (issue #230).
# Leave commented for ephemeral auto-assign (required for extra -p instances;
# the pin belongs to whichever single instance reads this .env).
# SH_PORT=3000
# SH_STORYBOOK_PORT=6006
# SH_HMR_PORT=24678

# ─── Supabase profile (docker compose --profile supabase up) ─────────────
# These host ports only matter when the `supabase` profile is active.
# Internal container-to-container traffic uses service DNS, not these ports.
SH_SUPABASE_API_PORT=54321
SH_DB_PORT=54322
SH_SUPABASE_STUDIO_PORT=54323
SH_SUPABASE_MAIL_PORT=54324
# Host port for the .NET messaging backend (profile: dotnet, #266/#265).
# SH_DOTNET_PORT=5099

# ─── Local Supabase (runs entirely inside Docker) ──────────────────────
# Start with: docker compose --profile supabase up
#
# These are standard Supabase demo keys from the self-hosting docs.
# They are NOT secrets. They match the docker-compose.yml defaults
# exactly, so no key generation is needed for local dev. Just
# uncomment the three lines below and comment out any cloud
# Supabase URL higher in your .env.
#
# The app container connects to Supabase via Docker DNS (supabase-kong
# on port 8000). Host ports are only for browser access to Studio.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=http://supabase-kong:8000
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyAgCiAgICAicm9sZSI6ICJhbm9uIiwKICAgICJpc3MiOiAic3VwYWJhc2UtZGVtbyIsCiAgICAiaWF0IjogMTY0MTc2OTIwMCwKICAgICJleHAiOiAxNzk5NTM1NjAwCn0.dc_X5iR_VP_qT0zsiyj_I_OZ2T9FtRU2BBNWN8Bu4GE
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyAgCiAgICAicm9sZSI6ICJzZXJ2aWNlX3JvbGUiLAogICAgImlzcyI6ICJzdXBhYmFzZS1kZW1vIiwKICAgICJpYXQiOiAxNjQxNzY5MjAwLAogICAgImV4cCI6IDE3OTk1MzU2MDAKfQ.DaYlNEoUrrEn2Ig7tqibS-PHK5vgusbcbo7X36XVt4Q

# Git Author Configuration (for commits from the Docker container)
#
# SET THESE. docker-compose.yml passes them into the container, and git inside
# the container has no other identity. Leave them unset and your commits go out
# with a placeholder author — GitHub matches commits to accounts by email, so
# they get credited to whichever stranger owns that address. That already
# happened here: 36 commits (2026-02-08..02-23) authored as dev@example.com are
# attributed on GitHub to an unrelated account, and history rewriting is the
# only way to undo it. See .mailmap.
#
# Use your GitHub noreply address to keep your real email out of public history
# (Settings > Emails > "Keep my email addresses private").
GIT_AUTHOR_NAME=Your Name
GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=your-username@users.noreply.github.com

# All variables in alphabetical order
# ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ANALYTICS_ENDPOINT=https://your-analytics-endpoint.com/api/vitals
# NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTHOR_AVATAR=https://example.com/avatar.png
# NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTHOR_BIO=Building modern web applications with Next.js
# NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTHOR_BLUESKY=username.bsky.social
# NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTHOR_EMAIL=you@example.com
# NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTHOR_GITHUB=GitHubUsername
# NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTHOR_LINKEDIN=https://www.linkedin.com/in/username/
# NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTHOR_MASTODON=@username@mastodon.social
# NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTHOR_NAME=Your Name
# NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTHOR_ROLE=Full Stack Developer
# NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTHOR_TWITCH=TwitchUsername
# NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTHOR_TWITTER=TwitterHandle
# NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTHOR_WEBSITE=https://yourwebsite.com
# NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTHOR_YOUTUBE=YouTubeHandle
# NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_PATH=/my-repo
# NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://yourdomain.com
# Messaging backend selector (#266): 'supabase' (default) or 'dotnet'.
# 'dotnet' routes the messaging domain through NEXT_PUBLIC_DOTNET_API_URL.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_BACKEND_PROVIDER=supabase
# CI=true
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CALENDAR_PROVIDER=calendly
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CALENDAR_URL=https://calendly.com/username/30min
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CASHAPP_CASHTAG=$yourcashtag
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CHIME_SIGN=$yourchimesign
# NEXT_PUBLIC_DEPLOY_URL=https://custom-domain.com
# NEXT_PUBLIC_DISQUS_SHORTNAME=yoursitename
# Base URL of the .NET messaging API (only used when NEXT_PUBLIC_BACKEND_PROVIDER=dotnet).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_DOTNET_API_URL=https://api.example.com
# Password for the least-privilege dotnet_app DB role the .NET backend logs in as (#321).
# Optional locally — defaults to POSTGRES_PASSWORD. Set a DISTINCT value in prod:
# the backend refuses to boot outside Development if it's the demo default.
# DOTNET_DB_PASSWORD=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_PUBLIC_KEY=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# NEXT_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_SERVICE_ID=service_xxxxxxx
# NEXT_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_TEMPLATE_ID=template_xxxxxxx
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID=G-XXXXXXXXXX
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_SITE_VERIFICATION=your-verification-token
# NODE_ENV=development
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PAGESPEED_API_KEY=your-google-api-key-here
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PAYPAL_CLIENT_ID=your-paypal-client-id
# Cloudflare Turnstile SITE key (browser-public). Sign-up bot protection is
# INERT until this is set (src/config/captcha.config.ts) — a fork with no value
# here ships a sign-up form with no bot protection. That form was previously
# abused to send confirmation email to non-consenting third parties (#353), so
# treat this as security config, not a nice-to-have. Pairs with TURNSTILE_SECRET.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CAPTCHA_SITE_KEY=0x4AAAAAAA...
# PayPal recurring billing Plan for the Subscribe flow (create a Product +
# Plan in the PayPal dashboard/API). Browser-public — amount/interval live on
# the Plan. Empty → the recurring PayPal path shows a "not configured" error.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PAYPAL_PLAN_ID — RETIRED (#774). One global plan id billed every
# recurring SKU the same amount, and the browser sending a plan at all was the
# tampering hole. The plan now comes from `products.paypal_plan_id` on the
# server, per SKU. Set that column, not an env var.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PROJECT_NAME=MyProject
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PROJECT_OWNER=MyUsername
# Sentry error monitoring (optional). Public client DSN from sentry.io → your
# project → Client Keys (DSN). Empty = monitoring disabled (no-op). Only sends
# after the user grants analytics consent.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=https://examplePublicKey@o0.ingest.us.sentry.io/0
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_TWITTER_HANDLE=TwitterHandle
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://yourdomain.com
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SOCIAL_PLATFORMS=github,linkedin,twitch,twitter
# A monitored address shown on /contact/ and offered when the contact FORM fails.
#
# UNSET = the mailto is not rendered anywhere, which is the correct default for a
# fork: publishing an inbox you do not own is the #392 failure. Set it to an address
# you actually read.
#
# This is NOT optional if you take payments. Stripe puts `support_url` on receipts,
# and ours points at /contact/ — a page that offered no working channel at all while
# NEXT_PUBLIC_WEB3FORMS_ACCESS_KEY was empty, because the form throws without it
# (#784). The form depends on a third-party credential; an address does not.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPPORT_EMAIL=support@yourdomain.com
#
# ─── Auth desired-state overrides, for FORKS (#734) ──────────────────────────
#
# `scripts/supabase/auth-config.json` is the DESIRED STATE that
# `auth-config-drift.yml` compares your live Supabase project against, daily and
# on every push to main. It ships ScriptHammer's own values as pinned defaults —
# a pinned literal is what makes drift detectable at all, because if the expected
# value came from the environment on both sides they could drift together and the
# gate could never fire (#287 is exactly that failure).
#
# So a fork does NOT edit that file. Set these instead and the defaults give way.
# They belong in CI as repo VARIABLES (Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions
# → Variables), not secrets: none is confidential, and both client ids already
# appear in every authorize URL the browser is redirected to.
#
# Leave them unset and the gate compares YOUR project against ScriptHammer's
# identity and fails. That is deliberate — it tells you to configure rather than
# passing silently.
#
# AUTH_SITE_URL=https://yourdomain.com
# AUTH_URI_ALLOW_LIST=https://yourdomain.com,https://yourdomain.com/**,http://localhost:3000,http://localhost:3000/**
# AUTH_SMTP_HOST=smtp.resend.com
# AUTH_SMTP_USER=resend
# AUTH_SMTP_ADMIN_EMAIL=noreply@yourdomain.com
# AUTH_SMTP_SENDER_NAME=YourProject
# AUTH_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=Ov23...
# AUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=000000000000-xxxxxxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com
# NO LONGER READ BY THE APP (#772). This was a single global Price id used for
# EVERY recurring SKU, so three Care Plan tiers at $49/$99/$249 would all have
# billed whatever it pointed at. Subscription prices now come from
# `products.stripe_price_id`, resolved server-side in create-stripe-subscription
# — the browser sends a product_id and never names a price.
#
# Still read by ONE local-only E2E fixture (tests/e2e/utils/test-user-factory.ts
# seedLiveStripeSubscription), which talks to Stripe directly to provision a test
# subscription. It is not a client value despite the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, and it
# is not needed to build or deploy.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PRICE_ID=price_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_test_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-supabase-anon-key
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
# NEXT_PUBLIC_WEB3FORMS_ACCESS_KEY=xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx

# Test User Configuration (for E2E and contract tests)
# Run: docker compose exec scripthammer pnpm exec tsx scripts/seed-test-users.ts
#
# IMPORTANT: Email Domain Requirements
# Supabase Auth validates that email domains have valid MX (mail exchange) records.
# DO NOT use:
#   - @example.com (reserved domain, always blocked)
#   - @yourdomain.com (blocked unless MX records configured)
#
# RECOMMENDED: Use Gmail plus aliases for test emails:
#   - yourname+test-a@gmail.com (primary)
#   - yourname+test-b@gmail.com (secondary)
#   - yourname+test-c@gmail.com (tertiary)
#
# The plus alias format (user+tag@gmail.com) allows unlimited unique emails.
# All emails arrive at yourname@gmail.com but Supabase treats each as unique.
#
# E2E Test Email Domain (REQUIRED for E2E tests)
# ⚠️  WITHOUT THIS, E2E TESTS WILL FAIL - Supabase rejects @example.com emails
# TEST_EMAIL_DOMAIN=yourname+e2e@gmail.com
#
# Primary test user - runs the E2E tests
# TEST_USER_PRIMARY_EMAIL=yourname+test-a@gmail.com
# TEST_USER_PRIMARY_PASSWORD=TestPassword123!
# Secondary test user - for multi-user E2E tests (connections, messaging)
# TEST_USER_SECONDARY_EMAIL=yourname+test-b@gmail.com
# TEST_USER_SECONDARY_PASSWORD=TestPassword456!
# Tertiary test user - for group chat tests (3+ members)
# TEST_USER_TERTIARY_EMAIL=yourname+test-c@gmail.com
# TEST_USER_TERTIARY_PASSWORD=TestPassword789!
# Admin user for system welcome messages (Feature 002/004)
# Admin public key is pre-stored in database - NO password needed at runtime
# See: scripts/seed-test-users.ts for admin setup
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ADMIN_USER_ID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001

# GitHub Personal Access Token (fine-grained)
# Generate at: https://github.com/settings/tokens?type=beta
# Needs: Actions (read) permission on this repository
# GH_TOKEN=github_pat_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# ─── Secret keys — READ BEFORE FILLING ────────────────────────────────
# NEVER put real values in .env.example (it's committed to git).
#
# For payment secrets (PAYPAL_*, STRIPE_*), .env is ALSO the wrong place.
# This project is a static export to GitHub Pages — there is no Next.js
# server runtime that can read non-NEXT_PUBLIC_ env vars at request time.
# Anything below without a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix belongs in **Supabase Vault**,
# accessed by Edge Functions via Deno.env.get(...).
#
# RECOMMENDED (CLI-free — this repo forbids installing the Supabase CLI locally):
#   1. cp scripts/supabase/edge-function-secrets.example.json \
#         scripts/supabase/edge-function-secrets.json   # gitignored
#   2. fill in STRIPE_SECRET_KEY / STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET / PAYPAL_CLIENT_SECRET /
#      PAYPAL_WEBHOOK_ID in that file
#   3. docker compose exec scripthammer pnpm supabase:secrets          # dry-run
#      docker compose exec scripthammer pnpm supabase:secrets --apply  # write
#   Uses SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN + project ref already in .env (Management API).
#
# Alternatives: `supabase secrets set NAME=value` (CLI) or the Supabase
# dashboard → Project Settings → Edge Functions → Secrets.
#
# Full guide: docs/PAYMENT-DEPLOYMENT.md
# Forker overview: README.md "Payment Integration Setup" section
#
# ─── Evaluation sessions / A-B testing ────────────────────────────────
#
# If you're running this repo as a code-agent evaluation snapshot and
# need to work on payment features WITHOUT real Stripe/PayPal keys:
#
#   1. Run `cp .env.example .env` to create your local .env
#   2. Set UID=$(id -u) and GID=$(id -g) in the new .env
#   3. Uncomment the NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL and anon key lines and fill
#      them with values from your Supabase project (or the local
#      docker-compose supabase profile — see the "Local Supabase" section
#      above for demo keys that are NOT secrets and are safe to use)
#   4. Leave ALL payment keys (NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY,
#      NEXT_PUBLIC_PAYPAL_CLIENT_ID, and the secrets below) empty
#
# The template already handles missing payment config gracefully (added
# in commit 77e409d, 2026-04-08): PaymentButton renders a "not configured"
# warning, /payment-demo shows a prominent banner, and provider tabs only
# render for configured providers. A model working on payment UI tasks
# can compose the components and observe the feature-flag gate WITHOUT
# needing real API keys. That's the expected eval mode — do not attempt
# to obtain real keys during an eval session.
#
# The entries below are placeholder names only — DO NOT add values here.
# (Exception: STRIPE_SECRET_KEY may ALSO live in the gitignored .env with a
# TEST-MODE key — the local-only subscription-lifecycle E2E fixture uses it to
# provision real test-mode subscriptions. Edge Functions still read it from
# Vault; CI has neither, so those tests auto-skip.)
#
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY MUST STAY TEST-MODE, and that is load-bearing.
# tests/e2e/utils/test-user-factory.ts reads it and PROVISIONS REAL
# SUBSCRIPTIONS; tests/e2e/payment/08-subscription-lifecycle.spec.ts runs
# whenever it is set. A live key in that variable means running the local E2E
# suite bills real money. There is no guard that catches this — the fixture
# cannot tell the two modes apart, because Stripe's API does not refuse.
#
# So a live secret key gets its OWN name, which nothing in the codebase reads:
#
#   STRIPE_SECRET_KEY_LIVE=sk_live_...   (gitignored .env only)
#
# It is an OPERATOR credential — for calling the Stripe API in live mode to read
# or set account settings (business_profile, webhook endpoints) without that key
# ever reaching a test fixture. Edge Functions get their live key from Supabase
# Vault, never from .env. See #629 (go-live) and #769 (support contact details).
# The UNPREFIXED PayPal client id, required IN ADDITION to the NEXT_PUBLIC_ one
# above. supabase/functions/resume-subscription and capture-paypal-order read
# `PAYPAL_CLIENT_ID` via Deno.env.get. Setting only the NEXT_PUBLIC_ variant has
# already caused a production 500 — set BOTH name variants.
# PAYPAL_CLIENT_ID=
# Cloudflare API token — OPERATOR CREDENTIAL, read by no application code.
#
# Used to move scripthammer.com onto Cloudflare (#635) and to stand up Email
# Routing for admin@scripthammer.com (#769). Nothing in src/ or
# supabase/functions/ reads it, so the #771 guard does not require it; it is
# documented because "no code reads it" is not grounds for leaving a credential
# undocumented — see the SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_* note below.
#
# Create at https://dash.cloudflare.com/profile/api-tokens -> Create Custom Token.
#
# Token name: `scripthammer.com DNS + Email Routing`
# Name it for the DOMAIN and the SCOPE. Cloudflare lists tokens by name and
# nothing else, so "My Token 3" is a token nobody will ever dare revoke. A name
# that says what it touches makes revocation a decision instead of a gamble.
#
# Permission rows. Every one of these was confirmed against the live API, not
# read off a docs page — two plausible-sounding claims did not survive that:
#
#   Zone    / DNS                     / Edit    (records, proxy status)
#   Zone    / Cache Rules             / Edit    (the #635 cache rules)
#   Zone    / Zone Settings           / Edit    (reads zone config, e.g. browser_cache_ttl)
#   Zone    / Email Routing Rules     / Edit    (the admin@ rule — ZONE scoped)
#   Account / Email Routing Addresses / Edit    (the destination inbox — ACCOUNT scoped)
#
# `Zone Settings / Edit` does NOT cover Cache Rules, however much it sounds like
# it should. With only that row, every `/rulesets` call returned `[10000]
# Authentication error`, and the #635 fix could not be applied at all. Cache
# Rules is a separate permission; grant it explicitly.
#
# Email Routing genuinely needs BOTH rows: Cloudflare splits the rule (zone) from
# the destination address (account). Miss either and admin@ half-works.
#
# ZONE CREATION IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* IN THIS TOKEN.
# `POST /zones` needs `com.cloudflare.api.account.zone.create`, and this token is
# scoped without it on purpose. Adding a domain is a rare, human decision with
# billing attached; a long-lived credential that can create sites in the account
# is a wider blast radius than the one click it would save. The zone is added in
# the dashboard, once, by a person — then this token manages the records, proxy
# status and Email Routing, which is the part that actually recurs and where
# mistakes are made.
#
# If a future change appears to "need" zone creation, that is the signal to ask
# why automation is creating domains, not to widen the token.
#
# Zone Resources starts as "All zones", because at creation time the zone does
# not exist yet and so cannot be named. Once it does exist, narrow the token to
# that single zone — "All zones" on a permanent credential is broader than the
# job needs, and the only reason for it was a bootstrap ordering problem that is
# over the moment the zone is added.
#
# DO pin Client IP filtering to your public IP. An earlier version of this note
# said to leave it empty, on the theory that WSL and Docker egress differently
# from the browser. They do not: all three leave through the same NAT, so a
# single address covers dashboard use and every API call made from a container.
# Confirmed by comparing the observed source address from WSL, from inside the
# container, and from the browser — identical. Re-pin it if the ISP rotates the
# address; narrowing a long-lived credential to one origin is worth that.
#
# Revocable in one click from the same page, which is why this is a token rather
# than a browser session.
# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=
# Where the contact form delivers, and the address it sends FROM (#784).
#
# These are EDGE FUNCTION secrets, not build-time values — set them on the Supabase
# project (Settings -> Edge Functions -> Secrets, or the Management API), not in the
# deploy workflow. `supabase/functions/contact-message` reads them via Deno.env.
#
# CONTACT_TO is read SERVER-SIDE and is never taken from the request. That is the
# security property, not a detail: an endpoint that lets the caller choose the
# recipient is an open relay, and this project has already had a form abused to
# email non-consenting third parties (#353).
#
# CONTACT_FROM must be an address on a domain VERIFIED in Resend, or delivery fails.
# Neither has a default: falling back to the upstream maintainer's domain would put
# their inbox behind every fork's contact form (#392) and send from a domain the
# fork does not own. Unset means the function returns 500 rather than pretending.
# CONTACT_TO=support@yourdomain.com
# CONTACT_FROM=Your Project <noreply@yourdomain.com>
# PayPal API base URL. DEFAULTS TO THE SANDBOX
# (https://api-m.sandbox.paypal.com) in supabase/functions/cancel-subscription
# and its siblings — so leaving this unset in production means cancellations and
# subscription calls silently talk to sandbox against live credentials. Set it to
# https://api-m.paypal.com for live.
# PAYPAL_API_BASE=
# PAYPAL_CLIENT_SECRET=
# PAYPAL_WEBHOOK_ID=
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY_LIVE=
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN=
#
# ⚠️ OAUTH CREDENTIAL RECORD — REFERENCED BY NO CODE, AND MUST NOT BE DELETED.
# These four are configured in the hosted Supabase dashboard, not read by this
# app, so a grep for usages finds nothing and they look dead. They are the local
# RECORD of those credentials. Supabase project config (OAuth + captcha secrets)
# has already been destroyed once here while the DATA was safely backed up, and
# it was unrecoverable. "No code references it" is not grounds to remove a
# credential. Naming follows supabase/config.toml, which uses the same
# convention for SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_APPLE_SECRET.
# SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
# SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GITHUB_SECRET=
# SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GOOGLE_SECRET=
# SUPABASE_DB_PASSWORD=
# SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF=
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
# Cloudflare Turnstile SECRET, the server half of NEXT_PUBLIC_CAPTCHA_SITE_KEY.
# Read by scripts/check-captcha.mjs and scripts/supabase/set-auth-config.ts; the
# verification script degrades to a warning without it. Supersedes the older
# CAPTCHA_SECRET_KEY name, which is dead (#771).
# TURNSTILE_SECRET=

# Grace window for the intake-upload orphan sweep (#560, T023). An unreferenced
# upload younger than this is never touched — a buyer who uploaded ninety seconds
# ago is still filling in the form. Read by the `sweep-intake-orphans` edge
# function; set it as a function secret, not here, to change it in production.
# INTAKE_ORPHAN_GRACE_DAYS=7
